## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. The comparison is shallow (`Object.is`), so **reference equality** determines whether a prop "changed."

Primitives compare by value — `"hello" === "hello"` is stable. Objects and functions compare by reference — a new object literal `{}` is always a new reference, even if contents are identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a new object reference → child always re-renders
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />

// Every render creates a new function reference → child always re-renders
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | When a child receives stable props but re-renders due to parent renders |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | When a value is expensive to compute, or when it's passed as a prop to a memoized child |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | When a function is passed as a prop to a memoized child |

**Rules of thumb:**
- `React.memo` is only effective if the props it receives are themselves stable.
- `useCallback` / `useMemo` without `React.memo` on the child is almost always pointless — React will re-render regardless.
- Don't reach for these by default. Profile first; memoization has its own overhead.

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

The most common mistake: passing a new object literal as the context value.

```jsx
// Bad — new object every render → ALL consumers re-render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Every time `Provider` re-renders (for any reason), a new `{ user, setUser }` object is created, invalidating every consumer.

**Fix:** memoize the value, or split into separate contexts (state vs. dispatch).

```jsx
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']);

  // New function reference every render
  const handleSelect = (item) => console.log('selected', item);

  // New array reference every render
  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.includes(query));

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = ({ items, onSelect }) => {
  // Re-renders on every keystroke even if items didn't change
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
};
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filtered` or `handleSelect` actually change:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [items] = useState(['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']);

  // Stable reference — only changes if dependency changes
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => console.log('selected', item), []);

  // Stable reference — only changes when items or query changes
  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.includes(query)),
    [items, query]
  );

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Opt into memoization — skips render if props are reference-equal
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(({ items, onSelect }) => {
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

**The key insight:** `React.memo` + stable props (`useCallback`/`useMemo`) work as a pair. Either one alone usually doesn't help.
